BOOKLET 4: RANDOM
DUNGEON GENERATION
Equipment
Pencil protractor, compass, ruler, dice, and graph
paper (approximately 40 - 42 by 50 - 54
squares).
Introduction
Most of the time, a solo player will decide that
the maximum size for a single level of a dungeon
is delimited by the edge of a page of graph
paper. If something rolled at random does not fit,
either adjust the size of a room, chamber, or
passage, or else re-roll. When acting as both the
player and Referee, one must always be fair.
A solo session can be used at a later time as
fodder for a multi-player campaign. A written
record of the session should note all baddies,
prizes, oddities, etc. encountered. Later, the
same situations can be used to entertain others
either as they play through the dungeon
themselves, or relive your exploits vicariously.
Designer Note: The original dungeon underneath
Blackmoor™ castle was analyzed using artificial
intelligence to extract the data used to build these
procedures. If followed, the type of dungeon
generated should more closely match that of the
Twin Cities campaign than the procedure later
written by Gary Gygax.
Starting Point
The Central Chamber (see below) and all
stairways should be located prior to creating
anything else. Once the stairways are mapped,
randomly determine which one the adventurer(s)
will use to access the first level to the dungeon.
•
 If the chosen stairway leads to the central
chamber, begin by numbering and
locating the other exits and stocking that
room.
•
 If it does not lead to the central chamber,
there is a 2-in-3 chance that the stairway
enters a room, otherwise it ends in a
passageway.
Exploring the Dungeon
For every 100 feet of passageway travelled (i.e.
every 10 squares), there is a 8-in-10 chance
that a room or chamber has been found. The
room or chamber is at the termination of the
passage (especially near the edge of the map) or
to either side. If the room is on the side of the
passage, first roll up the room with exits and then
determine which side. A room or chamber might
have to be positioned deliberately instead of
randomly.
A keyed list of rooms and their contents as well as
stairways and their direction can be kept on a 3"
x 5" card or two. On the map, mark each with a
number.
Every 2,000 square feet explored (i.e. every 20
squares) the solo player must roll for an
oddity. Optionally, the player could roll for an
oddity after every turn of exploration, assigning a
5% chance per square traversed. Oddities can
be denoted on the map with a letter key.
Designer Note: Whenever the term "squares" is
used, assume each square is 10' per side. If this
seems to generate too many oddities, the number
can be halved by changing the increment to 40
squares or the percentage to 2.5%.
Passageways
A passageway is built one section at a time
through dice rolls. The original direction of the
passage should be determined at random.
Likewise, if entering via a stairway, its position
and type should be randomized too. The
passageway's width stays the same throughout,
though branches may have a different width. A
passageway that "collides" with another feature
can either be transformed into a dead end, or a
secret door (even chances of either result). A
passageway that reaches the edge of the map
should dead end 5 to 10 feet prior to the map
edge.
