A 90° turn will be either to the right or left (even
chances of either result).
A diagonal turn requires a 1d6 roll: 1 - 2 left
45°; 3 - 4 right 45°; 5 behind left 135°; 6
behind right 135°.
For a T-junction or Y-junction, use best judgement
or dice (1d6: 1 - 2 bottom; 3 - 4 left; 5 - 6 right)
for which arm of the letter represents the
termination of the current passageway section.
There is also a 1-in-20 chance that the junction is
actually a 20' by 20' triangular room with exits
on all sides.
Whether a 4-way intersection is either a cross or
X-shaped depends on the direction of travel of the
current passageway section. If staggered, one or
more of the other arms of the 4-way intersection
will be no more than 10' distant.
For a radiating junction, there is a 8-in-10
chance it is a 5-way intersection and a 2-in-10
chance that it is a 6-way intersection. Again, use
best judgement or dice when deciding where the
current passage enters the junction.
Central Chamber
There is a 7-in-10 chance that a dungeon level
has a central chamber. This should be placed
on the map immediately. The location of each of
the exits should be determined as well as the first
section of each attached passage.
