Stairway - Surroundings
Any stairway in the first distance band is likely
found somewhere in the central chamber.
Otherwise roll 1d6:
1 or 2 - The stairway is in a passageway (or
alcove along a section of passageway). The
passageway's direction of travel dictates the
orientation of the stairway.
3 to 6 - The stairway is in a room or chamber
of 30' x 30' or less (keep rolling on the room
table until this result is obtained). The orientation
and exact location is left to the discretion of the
solo player.
Oddities
When an oddity has been found, there is a 4-in-6
chance that the next oddity is exactly the same.
This may create a thematic feel for portions of the
dungeon.
1. Animal Pens (5' square) or Dungeon Cells (10'
square)
2. 1d4 hanging cages with human(oid) bones in
them. Other implements of torture are in the
same area.
3. 1d8 circular or triangular pillars
4. Large, circular, load-bearing column
5. Pillar with secret door. A stairway is found
inside. The stairway may lead upwards or
downwards and ends in a small tunnel. This small
tunnel (3' - 5' wide and 5' - 6' high) is slightly
above or below the current dungeon level. The
tunnel leads to another secret door somewhere
else on the same level. Use the stairway
generation procedure to locate the exit.
6. Pit - See Traps above.
7. Fire Pit (30' x 20') - See Traps above.
8. ”Ghost Room" with 8 Ghosts, Haunting Spirits,
or Spectres The type of monster is appropriate to
the level of the dungeon.
9. 1d6 bodies - Bloated, blue, in rigor mortis,
etc. accompanied by copper coins and broken
weapons. Some bodies may be unique such as
that of a caveman (7' tall and hairy) or other
humanoid race.
10. Rubble (fills 3d3 squares)
11. Secret Door
12. Door (Ajar 3-in-4 chance; Closed 1-in-4
chance). Closed doors may be stuck, locked, or
both.
13. Pool of Water, Oblong - 10' x 15'. The water
is either potable or stagnant (even chances of
either result).
14. Underground Stream - Meanders 360 feet
across the map providing access to those who
can hold their breath. One hidden room is
connected to the stream, inaccessible in any other
way.
15. If oddity within an Irregular Cavern of 720
square feet or greater, there is a large
underground pool. Otherwise, re-roll.
16. Cage (see Traps above)
17. Slide (see Traps above)
18. Fallen statue of Poseidon (or similar object of
worship or adoration)
19. Living Statue. See BOOKLET 2.
20. Trap Door - Up 1d3 levels
21. Open Shaft - There is a 5-in-6 chance that
this just goes down 1d3 levels. There is a 1-in-6
chance that the shaft is essentially bottomless or
ends in hot lava.
22. Smoking Pot - Shoots balls of fire up to 20
feet that strike anyone on a roll of 7 or greater
on 2d6. Those struck receive 1d6 HTK of
damage with no chance to save.
23. Fountain with Devilish Statue - The life-sized,
obsidian devil has gold horns. Inlaid silver
highlights the nose, mouth, and ears. The ruby
eyes of the statue emit an ominous red glow.
From its mouth pours sulfurous (but drinkable)
water. If meddled with, the statue begins to howl
and the ground beings to tremble. If adventurers
persist, the area around the fountain collapses.
24. Glowing Stairway - Only one of these is
found within the dungeon. This 20' wide stairway
travels from the current level to the deepest one
with no chance to turn around once trod upon.
25. Glowing Bronze Tablet - The non-magical
writing upon it is in the common tongue. It can
say anything. The Referee may consult BOOKLET
10: ORACULAR TABLES to determine its subject.
26. Wooden bucket filled with blood.
27. 1d12 empty wooden caskets.
28. Invisible wall that blocks passage. Can be in
the middle of a room, passage, or even stairway.
29. A door leading to a 5' by 5' broom closet
(now empty) or privy.
30. Once sumptuous furnishings are found in
disrepair or ruin.
31. Notch in the wall containing a (magical)
statuette of a beautiful woman / handsome man.
Attempts to seduce possessor, waking him from
sleep. Treat each seduction as though the victim
has been subjected to a Charm Person spell. If
rebuffed, will transform into a Giant Snake and
attack.
32. Devil shaped indentation in the floor with an
altar flanked by flaming braziers. If disturbed, an
earthquake threatens a cave-in.
33. A patch of fungi of unknown type. The fungi
are very flammable. If inflamed, the smoke
produced may induce choking, vomiting,
hallucinations, or death.
34. The chamber, passage, or room is swept with
a powerful and sustained gust of air,
approximately 100 miles per hour. All torches
and lanterns are blown out. Any doors in the
vicinity are slammed open or shut (depending on
direction from the source of the wind). There is a
1-in-6 chance that the wind gust occurs when
adventurers pass by.
35. Spill
i. Uncut jewels - 1d100 jewels spill out of a
natural crack or pillar. They are worth 10 gp
each until cut and polished by a jeweler.
ii. Oil - This bubbles out of a crack in the wall or
ceiling and pools on the floor. It is suitable for
immediate use. There are 3d10 flasks worth.
iii. Steam - A natural vent in the wall, ceiling, or
floor emits high pressure steam at unpredictable
intervals. There is a 1-in-3 chance that anyone
passing by may be scalded for 1 die of damage.
iv. Tar - The floor is covered with bubbling tar.
Anyone passing through leaves footprints and
emits an unmistakeable odor for 1d6 turns.
Those in bare feet take 1 die of damage.
v. Vein of copper, silver, or gold is embedded in
the wall, floor, or ceiling. A Dwarf, Gnome, or
Fairy automatically spots it but others passing by
only have a 1-in-6 chance of noticing. The ore
requires mining, refining, and minting to be worth
anything.
36. A Gas Pocket of one of the following types.
Roll 1d8. Allow a saving throw.
i. Obscured vision for 1d6 squares
ii. Blinds those exposed for 1d6 turns
iii. Fear causes anyone exposed to run 1 normal
movement distance (i.e. 120' if unencumbered)
iv. Sleep 2d6 turns
v. A random Attribute is affected. Raised /
Lowered 1d3 points more for 1d6 hours
Sickness compels a return to the surface
vi. Deadly Poison
vii. Hypersensitive Vision - Anyone affected
(failed save) can detect the next secret door
automatically.
37. Haunting Spirit. See BOOKLET 2.
38. Minotaur. See BOOKLET 2.
Deeper Levels
For the second (and deeper) levels of a dungeon,
the Referee can either align the graph paper
exactly with the page above, or simply start in
the middle of a new sheet when adventurers
descend the stairs or fall down a chute, descend
an elevator, etc. In any case, it is necessary to
line up the vertical connections so the maps make
sense. Using a piece of tracing paper to do this
is helpful.
Soloists Unite!
To avoid encountering the same monsters,
oddities, and treasures, multiple solo-players can
exchange sealed envelopes. Another solo player
could even provide for a whole series of rooms,
new artifacts, etc. The outside of the envelope
should be marked with the level of the dungeon
for which the contents are meant. It might include
a descriptive word such as "triangular room" or
"trapped passageway" so the recipient can
ensure the envelope is only opened if it matches
the current state of a solo session.
MONSTERS
The number of monsters is at rough parity in HTK
with the adventuring party when playing solo.
There is an 1-in-6 chance that there should be
one less or one more creature to weaken or
strengthen the encounter (even chances of either
result). This is true even in cases where a
multiplier is indicated (qty x 2 or x 3). Sometimes
HTK may be halved or doubled if only a single
